<section number="10.1" title="Solve Quadratic Equations Using the Square Root Property">
  <learning_objectives>
    <objective number="" />
    <objective number="" />
  </learning_objectives>
  <note type="Be Prepared" number="10.1">
    <p>Before you get started, take this readiness quiz.</p>
    <p>Simplify: $$\sqrt{75}$$.
If you missed this problem, review Example 9.12.</p>
  </note>
  <note type="Be Prepared" number="10.2">
    <p>Simplify: $$\sqrt{\frac{64}{3}}$$.
If you missed this problem, review Example 9.67.</p>
  </note>
  <note type="Be Prepared" number="10.3">
    <p>Factor: $$4x^{2} - 12x + 9$$.
If you missed this problem, review Example 7.43.</p>
  </note>
  <p>Quadratic equations are equations of the form $$ax^{2} + bx + c = 0$$, where $$a \neq 0$$. They differ from linear equations by including a term with the variable raised to the second power. We use different methods to solve quadratic equations than linear equations, because just adding, subtracting, multiplying, and dividing terms will not isolate the variable.</p>
  <p>We have seen that some quadratic equations can be solved by factoring. In this chapter, we will use three other methods to solve quadratic equations.</p>
  <subsection title="Solve Quadratic Equations of the Formax2=kUsing the Square Root Property">
    <p>We have already solved some quadratic equations by factoring. Let’s review how we used factoring to solve the quadratic equation $$x^{2} = 9$$.</p>
    <equation>$$\begin{array}{cccrlr} &amp; &amp; &amp; x^{2} &amp; = &amp; 9 \\ \text{Put the equation in standard form.} &amp; &amp; &amp; {x^{2} - 9} &amp; = &amp; 0 \\ \text{Factor the left side.} &amp; &amp; &amp; {\left( {x - 3} \right)\left( {x + 3} \right)} &amp; = &amp; 0 \\ \text{Use the Zero Product Property.} &amp; &amp; &amp; {\left( {x - 3} \right) = 0,\mspace{9mu}\left( {x + 3} \right)} &amp; = &amp; 0 \\ \text{Solve each equation.} &amp; &amp; &amp; {x = 3,\mspace{50mu} x} &amp; = &amp; -3 \\ {\text{Combine the two solutions into}\ \pm \ \text{form.}} &amp; &amp; &amp; x &amp; = &amp; {\pm \ 3} \\ {\text{(The solution is read}\ ‘x\ \text{is equal to positive or negative 3.’)}} &amp; &amp; &amp; &amp; &amp; \end{array}$$</equation>
    <p>We can easily use factoring to find the solutions of similar equations, like $$x^{2} = 16$$ and $$x^{2} = 25$$, because 16 and 25 are perfect squares. But what happens when we have an equation like $$x^{2} = 7$$? Since 7 is not a perfect square, we cannot solve the equation by factoring.</p>
    <p>These equations are all of the form $$x^{2} = k$$.We defined the square root of a number in this way:</p>
    <equation>$$\text{If}\ n^{2} = m,\ \text{then}\ n\ \text{is a square root of}\ m.$$</equation>
    <p>This leads to the Square Root Property.</p>
    <note type="Square Root Property">
      <p>If $$x^{2} = k$$, and $$k \geq 0$$, then $$x = \sqrt{k}\ \text{or}\ x = \text{−}\sqrt{k}$$.</p>
    </note>
    <p>Notice that the Square Root Property gives two solutions to an equation of the form $$x^{2} = k$$: the principal square root of $$k$$ and its opposite. We could also write the solution as $$x = \pm \ \sqrt{k}$$.</p>
    <p>Now, we will solve the equation $$x^{2} = 9$$ again, this time using the Square Root Property.</p>
    <equation>$$\begin{array}{cccrll} &amp; &amp; &amp; x^{2} &amp; = &amp; 9 \\ \text{Use the Square Root Property.} &amp; &amp; &amp; x &amp; = &amp; {\pm \ \sqrt{9}} \\ \text{Simplify the radical.} &amp; &amp; &amp; x &amp; = &amp; {\pm \ 3} \\ \text{Rewrite to show the two solutions.} &amp; &amp; &amp; {x = 3,x} &amp; = &amp; -3 \end{array}$$</equation>
    <p>What happens when the constant is not a perfect square? Let’s use the Square Root Property to solve the equation $$x^{2} = 7$$.</p>
    <equation>$$\begin{array}{lccc} \begin{array}{l} \\ \\ \\ \text{Use the Square Root Property.} \end{array} &amp; &amp; &amp; \begin{array}{rcl} x^{2} &amp; = &amp; 7 \\ x &amp; = &amp; {\pm \ \sqrt{7}} \end{array} \\ \text{Rewrite to show two solutions.} &amp; &amp; &amp; {x = \sqrt{7},\quad x = \text{−}\sqrt{7}} \\ {\text{We cannot simplify}\ \sqrt{7},\ \text{so we leave the answer as a radical.}} &amp; &amp; &amp; \end{array}$$</equation>
    <example number="10.1">
      <problem>
        <p>Solve: $$x^{2} = 169$$.</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <p>$$\begin{array}{lccc} \begin{array}{l} \\ \\ \\ \text{Use the Square Root Property.} \\ \text{Simplify the radical.} \end{array} &amp; &amp; &amp; {\mspace{72mu}\begin{array}{rll} x^{2} &amp; = &amp; 169 \\ x &amp; = &amp; {\pm \ \sqrt{169}} \\ x &amp; = &amp; {\pm \ 13} \end{array}} \\ \text{Rewrite to show two solutions.} &amp; &amp; &amp; {\mspace{72mu} x = 13,\quad x = -13} \end{array}$$</p>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="10.1">
      <p>Solve: $$x^{2} = 81$$.</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="10.2">
      <p>Solve: $$y^{2} = 121$$.</p>
    </note>
    <example number="10.2">
      <problem>
        <p>Solve: $$x^{2} - 48 = 0$$.</p>
      </problem>
      <solution />
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="10.3">
      <p>Solve: $$x^{2} - 50 = 0$$.</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="10.4">
      <p>Solve: $$y^{2} - 27 = 0$$.</p>
    </note>
    <note type="How To">
      <list type="ol" style="1">
        <item>Step 1. 
Isolate the quadratic term and make its coefficient one.</item>
        <item>Step 2. 
Use Square Root Property.</item>
        <item>Step 3. 
Simplify the radical.</item>
        <item>Step 4. 
Check the solutions.</item>
      </list>
    </note>
    <p>To use the Square Root Property, the coefficient of the variable term must equal 1. In the next example, we must divide both sides of the equation by 5 before using the Square Root Property.</p>
    <example number="10.3">
      <problem>
        <p>Solve: $$5m^{2} = 80$$.</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <table>
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell>The quadratic term is isolated.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{2mu} 5m^{2} = 80$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Divide by 5 to make its cofficient 1.</cell>
              <cell>$$\frac{5m^{2}}{5} = \frac{80}{5}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{11mu} m^{2} = 16$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Use the Square Root Property.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{20mu} m = \pm \ \sqrt{16}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify the radical.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{20mu} m = \pm \ 4$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Rewrite to show two solutions.</cell>
              <cell>$$m = 4,m = \text{−}4$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Check the solutions.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="10.5">
      <p>Solve: $$2x^{2} = 98$$.</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="10.6">
      <p>Solve: $$3z^{2} = 108$$.</p>
    </note>
    <p>The Square Root Property started by stating, ‘If $$x^{2} = k$$, and $$k \geq 0$$’. What will happen if $$k &lt; 0$$? This will be the case in the next example.</p>
    <example number="10.4">
      <problem>
        <p>Solve: $$q^{2} + 24 = 0$$.</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <table summary="Demonstrates solving the quadratic equation q^2 + 24 = 0, illustrating steps that lead to the conclusion of no real solution.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$q^{2} + 24 = 0$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Isolate the quadratic term.</cell>
              <cell>$$\qquad q^{2} = -24$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Use the Square Root Property.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{45mu} q = \pm \ \sqrt{-24}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>The $$\sqrt{-24}$$ is not a real number.</cell>
              <cell>There is no real solution.</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="10.7">
      <p>Solve: $$c^{2} + 12 = 0$$.</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="10.8">
      <p>Solve: $$d^{2} + 81 = 0$$.</p>
    </note>
    <p>Remember, we first isolate the quadratic term and then make the coefficient equal to one.</p>
    <example number="10.5">
      <problem>
        <p>Solve: $$\frac{2}{3}u^{2} + 5 = 17$$.</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <table>
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\frac{2}{3}u^{2} + 5 = 17$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Isolate the quadratic term.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{31mu}\frac{2}{3}u^{2} = 12$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Multiply by $$\frac{3}{2}$$ to make the coefficient 1.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{9mu}\frac{3}{2} \cdot \frac{2}{3}u^{2} = \frac{3}{2} \cdot 12$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{40mu} u^{2} = 18$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Use the Square Root Property.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{49mu} u = \pm \ \sqrt{18}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify the radical.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{49mu} u = \pm \ \sqrt{9}\sqrt{2}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{49mu} u = \pm \ 3\sqrt{2}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Rewrite to show two solutions.</cell>
              <cell>$$u = 3\sqrt{2},u = \text{−}3\sqrt{2}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Check.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="10.9">
      <p>Solve: $$\frac{1}{2}x^{2} + 4 = 24$$.</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="10.10">
      <p>Solve: $$\frac{3}{4}y^{2} - 3 = 18$$.</p>
    </note>
    <p>The solutions to some equations may have fractions inside the radicals. When this happens, we must rationalize the denominator.</p>
    <example number="10.6">
      <problem>
        <p>Solve: $$2c^{2} - 4 = 45$$.</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <table summary="Step-by-step solution of a quadratic equation using the Square Root Property.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell>Isolate the quadratic term.Divide by 2 to make the coefficient 1.Simplify.Use the Square Root Property.Simplify the radical.Rationalize the denominator.Simplify.</cell>
              <cell>$$\begin{array}{rll} &amp; &amp; \\ {\mspace{72mu} 2c^{2} - 4} &amp; = &amp; 45 \\ {\mspace{72mu} 2c^{2}} &amp; = &amp; 49 \\ {\mspace{72mu}\frac{2c^{2}}{2}} &amp; = &amp; \frac{49}{2} \\ {\mspace{72mu} c^{2}} &amp; = &amp; \frac{49}{2} \\ {\mspace{72mu} c} &amp; = &amp; {\pm \ \sqrt{\frac{49}{2}}} \\ {\mspace{72mu} c} &amp; = &amp; {\pm \ \frac{\sqrt{49}}{\sqrt{2}}} \\ {\mspace{72mu} c} &amp; = &amp; {\pm \ \frac{\sqrt{49} \cdot \sqrt{2}}{\sqrt{2} \cdot \sqrt{2}}} \\ {\mspace{72mu} c} &amp; = &amp; {\pm \ \frac{7\sqrt{2}}{2}} \end{array}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Rewrite to show two solutions.</cell>
              <cell>$$c = \frac{7\sqrt{2}}{2},\quad c = - \frac{7\sqrt{2}}{2}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Check. We leave the check for you.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="10.11">
      <p>Solve: $$5r^{2} - 2 = 34$$.</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="10.12">
      <p>Solve: $$3t^{2} + 6 = 70$$.</p>
    </note>
  </subsection>
  <subsection title="Solve Quadratic Equations of the Forma(x−h)2=kUsing the Square Root Property">
    <p>We can use the Square Root Property to solve an equation like $$\left( {x - 3} \right)^{2} = 16$$, too. We will treat the whole binomial, $$\left( {x - 3} \right)$$, as the quadratic term.</p>
    <example number="10.7">
      <problem>
        <p>Solve: $$\left( {x - 3} \right)^{2} = 16$$.</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <table>
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$(x - 3)^{2} = 16$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Use the Square Root Property.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{20mu} x - 3 = \pm \ \sqrt{16}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{20mu} x - 3 = \pm \ 4$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Write as two equations.</cell>
              <cell>$$x - 3 = 4,x - 3 = \text{−}4$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Solve.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{29mu} x = 7,x = \text{−}1$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Check.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="10.13">
      <p>Solve: $$\left( {q + 5} \right)^{2} = 1$$.</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="10.14">
      <p>Solve: $$\left( {r - 3} \right)^{2} = 25$$.</p>
    </note>
    <example number="10.8">
      <problem>
        <p>Solve: $$\left( {y - 7} \right)^{2} = 12$$.</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <table>
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$(y - 7)^{2} = 12$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Use the Square Root Property.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{16mu} y - 7 = \pm \ \sqrt{12}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify the radical.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{16mu} y - 7 = \pm \ 2\sqrt{3}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Solve for y.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{45mu} y = 7 \pm 2\sqrt{3}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Rewrite to show two solutions.</cell>
              <cell>$$y = 7 + 2\sqrt{3},y = 7 - 2\sqrt{3}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Check.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="10.15">
      <p>Solve: $$\left( {a - 3} \right)^{2} = 18$$.</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="10.16">
      <p>Solve: $$\left( {b + 2} \right)^{2} = 40$$.</p>
    </note>
    <p>Remember, when we take the square root of a fraction, we can take the square root of the numerator and denominator separately.</p>
    <example number="10.9">
      <problem>
        <p>Solve: $$\left( {x - \frac{1}{2}} \right)^{2} = \frac{5}{4}.$$</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <table summary="This table illustrates the step-by-step solution of a quadratic equation using the Square Root Property.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell>Use the Square Root Property.Rewrite the radical as a fraction of square roots.Simplify the radical.Solve for x.</cell>
              <cell>$$\begin{array}{rll} &amp; &amp; \\ \left( {x - \frac{1}{2}} \right)^{2} &amp; = &amp; \frac{5}{4} \\ {x - \frac{1}{2}} &amp; = &amp; {\pm \ \sqrt{\frac{5}{4}}} \\ {x - \frac{1}{2}} &amp; = &amp; {\pm \ \frac{\sqrt{5}}{\sqrt{4}}} \\ {x - \frac{1}{2}} &amp; = &amp; {\pm \ \frac{\sqrt{5}}{2}} \\ x &amp; = &amp; {\frac{1}{2} \pm \frac{\sqrt{5}}{2}} \end{array}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Rewrite to show two solutions.</cell>
              <cell>$$x = \frac{1}{2} + \frac{\sqrt{5}}{2},\quad x = \frac{1}{2} - \frac{\sqrt{5}}{2}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Check. We leave the check for you.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="10.17">
      <p>Solve: $$\left( {x - \frac{1}{3}} \right)^{2} = \frac{5}{9}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="10.18">
      <p>Solve: $$\left( {y - \frac{3}{4}} \right)^{2} = \frac{7}{16}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <p>We will start the solution to the next example by isolating the binomial.</p>
    <example number="10.10">
      <problem>
        <p>Solve: $$\left( {x - 2} \right)^{2} + 3 = 30$$.</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <table summary="Step-by-step solution to a quadratic equation using the Square Root Property.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\mspace{23mu}\begin{array}{rll} &amp; &amp; \\ {\left( {x - 2} \right)^{2} + 3} &amp; = &amp; 30 \end{array}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Isolate the binomial term.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{54mu}\begin{array}{rll} &amp; &amp; \\ \left( {x - 2} \right)^{2} &amp; = &amp; 27 \end{array}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Use the Square Root Property.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{77mu}\begin{array}{rll} &amp; &amp; \\ {x - 2} &amp; = &amp; {\pm \ \sqrt{27}} \end{array}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify the radical.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{77mu}\begin{array}{rll} &amp; &amp; \\ {x - 2} &amp; = &amp; {\pm \ 3\sqrt{3}} \end{array}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Solve for x.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{108mu}\begin{array}{rll} &amp; &amp; \\ x &amp; = &amp; {2 \pm 3\sqrt{3}} \end{array}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Rewrite to show two solutions.</cell>
              <cell>$$\qquad x = 2 + 3\sqrt{3},\quad x = 2 - 3\sqrt{3}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Check. We leave the check for you.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="10.19">
      <p>Solve: $$\left( {a - 5} \right)^{2} + 4 = 24$$.</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="10.20">
      <p>Solve: $$\left( {b - 3} \right)^{2} - 8 = 24$$.</p>
    </note>
    <example number="10.11">
      <problem>
        <p>Solve: $$\left( {3v - 7} \right)^{2} = -12$$.</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <table summary="Demonstration of applying the square root property to solve (3v-7)^2 = -12, resulting in no real solution.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell>Use the Square Root Property.</cell>
              <cell>$$\begin{array}{rll} &amp; &amp; \\ \left( {3v - 7} \right)^{2} &amp; = &amp; -12 \\ {3v - 7} &amp; = &amp; {\pm \ \sqrt{-12}} \end{array}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>The $$\sqrt{-12}$$ is not a real number.</cell>
              <cell>There is no real solution.</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="10.21">
      <p>Solve: $$\left( {3r + 4} \right)^{2} = -8$$.</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="10.22">
      <p>Solve: $$\left( {2t - 8} \right)^{2} = -10$$.</p>
    </note>
    <p>The left sides of the equations in the next two examples do not seem to be of the form $$a\left( {x - h} \right)^{2}$$. But they are perfect square trinomials, so we will factor to put them in the form we need.</p>
    <example number="10.12">
      <problem>
        <p>Solve: $$p^{2} - 10p + 25 = 18$$.</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <p>The left side of the equation is a perfect square trinomial. We will factor it first.</p>
        <table summary="Demonstrates solving a perfect square trinomial using the Square Root Property, showing steps like factoring, simplifying radicals, and presenting two solutions for p.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell>Factor the perfect square trinomial.Use the Square Root Property.Simplify the radical.Solve for p.</cell>
              <cell>$$\begin{array}{rll} &amp; &amp; \\ {p^{2} - 10p + 25} &amp; = &amp; 18 \\ &amp; &amp; \\ \left( {p - 5} \right)^{2} &amp; = &amp; 18 \\ &amp; &amp; \\ {p - 5} &amp; = &amp; {\pm \ \sqrt{18}} \\ &amp; &amp; \\ {p - 5} &amp; = &amp; {\pm \ 3\sqrt{2}} \\ &amp; &amp; \\ p &amp; = &amp; {5 \pm 3\sqrt{2}} \end{array}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Rewrite to show two solutions.</cell>
              <cell>$$p = 5 + 3\sqrt{2,}\quad p = 5 - 3\sqrt{2}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Check. We leave the check for you.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="10.23">
      <p>Solve: $$x^{2} - 6x + 9 = 12$$.</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="10.24">
      <p>Solve: $$y^{2} + 12y + 36 = 32$$.</p>
    </note>
    <example number="10.13">
      <problem>
        <p>Solve: $$4n^{2} + 4n + 1 = 16$$.</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <p>Again, we notice the left side of the equation is a perfect square trinomial. We will factor it first.</p>
        <table>
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$4n^{2} + 4n + 1 = 16$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Factor the perfect square trinomial.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{27mu}(2n + 1)^{2} = 16$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Use the Square Root Property.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{47mu} 2n + 1 = \pm \ \sqrt{16}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify the radical.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{47mu} 2n + 1 = \pm \ 4$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Solve for n.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{76mu} 2n = \text{−}1 \pm 4$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Divide each side by 2.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{65mu}\begin{array}{rll} \frac{2n}{2} &amp; = &amp; \frac{- 1 \pm 4}{2} \\ n &amp; = &amp; \frac{- 1 \pm 4}{2} \end{array}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Rewrite to show two solutions.</cell>
              <cell>$$n = \frac{- 1 + 4}{2},n = \frac{- 1 - 4}{2}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify each equation.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{43mu} n = \frac{3}{2},n = - \frac{5}{2}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Check.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
